WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.130 --> 00:00:03.260
He's just sort of part
of the musical furniture

00:00:03.260 --> 00:00:04.920
of American culture.

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:06.930
It's just wallpaper.

00:00:06.930 --> 00:00:09.860
With Kenny G's music, what can you say?

00:00:09.860 --> 00:00:12.740
Kenny G, the curly-maned saxophonist

00:00:12.740 --> 00:00:16.170
who has sold 75 million
records and inspired

00:00:16.170 --> 00:00:19.130
the entire genre of smooth jazz

00:00:19.130 --> 00:00:22.310
isn't just the best-selling
instrumentalist of all time.

00:00:22.310 --> 00:00:24.770
He's also one of the
most critically reviled

00:00:24.770 --> 00:00:26.230
musicians in history.

00:00:26.230 --> 00:00:29.277
And he's the subject of
the new HBO documentary,

00:00:29.277 --> 00:00:32.030
"Listening to Kenny G,"
which The New Yorker called

00:00:32.030 --> 00:00:34.960
an ironic masterpiece
that perfectly defines

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:38.190
the elusive and contested
concept of a guilty pleasure.

00:00:38.190 --> 00:00:41.100
In "Listening to Kenny
G," director Penny Lane

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:44.790
explores why critics hate Kenny
G and the masses love him,

00:00:44.790 --> 00:00:48.210
creating a funny poignant
and entrancing conversation

00:00:48.210 --> 00:00:51.230
about mass commercial
appeal and elite tastes.

00:00:51.230 --> 00:00:53.210
When I think about music,

00:00:53.210 --> 00:00:54.690
the first thing that comes to mind

00:00:54.690 --> 00:00:58.670
is just the idea of taste and
how deeply intimate that is

00:00:58.670 --> 00:01:02.580
with our sense of selves and
your sense of social identity.

00:01:02.580 --> 00:01:04.490
So I really wanted to
do something about that.

00:01:04.490 --> 00:01:07.540
And then getting from that
to Kenny was pretty obvious.

00:01:07.540 --> 00:01:12.070
So an exploration of why
Kenny G is the most popular

00:01:12.070 --> 00:01:15.470
and successful and best-selling
instrumentalist of all time

00:01:15.470 --> 00:01:17.690
and why that success makes

00:01:17.690 --> 00:01:19.940
a certain subset of people really mad.

00:01:20.817 --> 00:01:24.160
That's a hard lick. I just
played it really well.

00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:25.890
Lane gives the critics their due.

00:01:25.890 --> 00:01:29.050
To many of them, Kenny G is
offensive because he seems

00:01:29.050 --> 00:01:32.290
fundamentally uninterested
in the musical tradition

00:01:32.290 --> 00:01:35.340
that he has come to define
for millions of people

00:01:35.340 --> 00:01:36.173
around the globe.

00:01:36.173 --> 00:01:37.006
I spent a lot of time with Kenny

00:01:37.950 --> 00:01:40.460
and it kinda didn't matter
how many different ways

00:01:40.460 --> 00:01:42.870
I asked him what he was up to

00:01:42.870 --> 00:01:44.290
when he was creating these songs.

00:01:44.290 --> 00:01:46.530
He was just like, "I don't know.

00:01:46.530 --> 00:01:48.230
It's pretty. I like it."

00:01:48.230 --> 00:01:50.410
Most artists have a whole set of things

00:01:50.410 --> 00:01:52.560
they're thinking about,
like who your audience is,

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:53.760
and who you're in conversation with,

00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:55.440
and what histories you're drawing on.

00:01:55.440 --> 00:01:58.160
How are you innovating? How
are you making something new?

00:01:58.160 --> 00:01:59.960
Kenny just isn't engaged in that.

00:01:59.960 --> 00:02:04.960
And I think it's his utter
lack of interest in jazz.

00:02:06.490 --> 00:02:08.750
Kenny G does not know anything about jazz.

00:02:08.750 --> 00:02:11.253
So what do the people
like who love Kenny G?

00:02:11.253 --> 00:02:12.860
I think the most important thing

00:02:12.860 --> 00:02:15.030
is that it's music that is useful.

00:02:15.030 --> 00:02:17.010
It's useful in their lives.

00:02:17.010 --> 00:02:19.920
I think life is hard and
there's is nothing wrong

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:22.110
with wanting something to relax to

00:02:22.110 --> 00:02:24.980
after your 10 hours at
the factory or whatever.

00:02:24.980 --> 00:02:27.270
It's useful music because
it doesn't have lyrics.

00:02:27.270 --> 00:02:28.280
It doesn't get in the way.

00:02:28.280 --> 00:02:29.780
You can put it on in the background.

00:02:29.780 --> 00:02:32.640
So people study to it,
they meditate to it.

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:35.490
They run to it. They make love to it.

00:02:35.490 --> 00:02:37.180
They get married to it.

00:02:37.180 --> 00:02:39.300
They've used it to put babies to sleep.

00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:41.750
Anesthesiologists use it before surgery.

00:02:41.750 --> 00:02:43.910
It helps get the heart rate down.

00:02:43.910 --> 00:02:47.580
The Chinese government
uses it to send people home

00:02:47.580 --> 00:02:48.740
at the end of the workday.

00:02:48.740 --> 00:02:50.960
It is a supremely functional music,

00:02:50.960 --> 00:02:53.310
which is interesting
because it's referred to

00:02:53.310 --> 00:02:55.450
by another critic as musical furniture,

00:02:55.450 --> 00:02:57.290
which I think is actually pretty apt.

00:02:57.290 --> 00:03:00.020
It's easy to forget that most people,

00:03:00.020 --> 00:03:01.560
when I tell them that I made a film

00:03:01.560 --> 00:03:03.807
about why people hate Kenny G are like

00:03:03.807 --> 00:03:05.980
"Who hates Kenny G?

00:03:05.980 --> 00:03:07.850
Everyone loves Kenny G!"

00:03:07.850 --> 00:03:09.760
Recent interviewed you a few years ago

00:03:09.760 --> 00:03:13.690
when your incredible documentary
"Hail Satan?" came out.

00:03:13.690 --> 00:03:17.480
That is a film about a group
of Satanists who are pushing

00:03:17.480 --> 00:03:20.690
for equal representation
of religious icons

00:03:20.690 --> 00:03:21.810
in the public square.

00:03:21.810 --> 00:03:23.010
What's the connection there?

00:03:23.010 --> 00:03:25.020
The joke way of putting it would be to say

00:03:25.020 --> 00:03:28.940
after I made a movie defending Satanists,

00:03:28.940 --> 00:03:30.777
I thought to myself

00:03:30.777 --> 00:03:34.720
"Who do people hate more than Satanists?"

00:03:34.720 --> 00:03:36.040
But that's not really true, of course.

00:03:36.040 --> 00:03:39.500
It's more like I am I
think what I'm good at

00:03:39.500 --> 00:03:41.940
and what I think what I'm good at

00:03:41.940 --> 00:03:44.350
and what I'm trying to
do often with my films

00:03:44.350 --> 00:03:49.350
is find really entertaining
funny ways to talk

00:03:49.590 --> 00:03:52.200
about really serious
issues that I don't think

00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:56.060
a poe-faced delivery would
get a big audience for.

00:03:56.060 --> 00:03:59.070
And I want people to be
able to change their minds.

00:03:59.070 --> 00:04:02.560
I want people to be willing
to change their minds

00:04:02.560 --> 00:04:03.660
and I love that.

00:04:03.660 --> 00:04:06.680
I love the feeling of
finding out that I'm wrong.

00:04:06.680 --> 00:04:09.200
Not everyone does. I love it.

00:04:09.200 --> 00:04:10.900
I'm trying to engineer moments

00:04:10.900 --> 00:04:11.733
where it's like destabilizing for people.

00:04:11.733 --> 00:04:12.566
I think one of the most
insightful criticisms

00:04:14.990 --> 00:04:18.410
is a jazz ensemble is kind
of a call and response thing,

00:04:18.410 --> 00:04:21.580
it's equal parts, everybody's
playing against each other,

00:04:21.580 --> 00:04:23.520
and that Kenny G, it's just Kenny G.

00:04:23.520 --> 00:04:24.590
It's just the saxophone.

00:04:24.590 --> 00:04:26.740
Everybody else there is
there to support him.

00:04:26.740 --> 00:04:27.696
Yeah, it's a solo-

00:04:27.696 --> 00:04:29.520
He says, "It's not sex.
It's masturbation."

00:04:29.520 --> 00:04:31.387
The most quoted line in the movie.

00:04:31.387 --> 00:04:32.810
But that guy is also

00:04:32.810 --> 00:04:35.580
a ridiculous character of a jazz.

00:04:35.580 --> 00:04:38.130
Why should jazz have one meaning

00:04:38.130 --> 00:04:41.370
that is described by some old white guys

00:04:41.370 --> 00:04:45.360
who are really upset that
this Jewish guy from Seattle

00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:46.710
is selling so many records

00:04:46.710 --> 00:04:50.200
and is like fucking with their little pot.

00:04:50.200 --> 00:04:51.033
Yes.

00:04:51.033 --> 00:04:54.460
I agree with you that that is
a question the movie raises.

00:04:54.460 --> 00:04:55.590
I can see it both ways.

00:04:55.590 --> 00:04:56.860
I'm trying to play down the middle.

00:04:56.860 --> 00:04:58.900
I'm trying to like lay out

00:04:58.900 --> 00:05:02.430
a kind of like banquet
of discussion topics.

00:05:02.430 --> 00:05:04.610
Lane's film's all proceed
from the assumption

00:05:04.610 --> 00:05:06.350
that she doesn't have all the answers

00:05:06.350 --> 00:05:08.050
and that she doesn't know the best way

00:05:08.050 --> 00:05:11.490
for other people to live or
the best music to listen to.

00:05:11.490 --> 00:05:13.050
It's no coincidence that she's

00:05:13.050 --> 00:05:15.602
a longtime reader of Reason magazine.

00:05:15.602 --> 00:05:17.880
I started subscribing to
Reason when I was in college

00:05:17.880 --> 00:05:19.000
and I went to Vassar.

00:05:19.000 --> 00:05:20.530
So it like came in the mail.

00:05:20.530 --> 00:05:22.750
I would like put it
underneath all my other mail

00:05:22.750 --> 00:05:24.140
to bring it back to my dorm room.

00:05:24.140 --> 00:05:25.810
But honestly, in high school,

00:05:25.810 --> 00:05:27.680
I remember we had a politics class

00:05:27.680 --> 00:05:30.520
and you were supposed to
choose Democrat or Republican

00:05:30.520 --> 00:05:32.850
to be part of the debate or whatever.

00:05:32.850 --> 00:05:35.530
And I just like didn't like it,

00:05:35.530 --> 00:05:36.570
I just didn't know what to do,

00:05:36.570 --> 00:05:38.150
'cause I was like, "I
don't want either one."

00:05:38.150 --> 00:05:39.017
And my teacher was like,

00:05:39.017 --> 00:05:41.410
"Okay, well there's this other thing."

00:05:41.410 --> 00:05:44.240
And I think on some level I
probably was attracted to it

00:05:44.240 --> 00:05:46.390
because it made me feel special.

00:05:46.390 --> 00:05:48.310
I'm the one person here who doesn't wanna

00:05:48.310 --> 00:05:49.270
go along with the crowd.

00:05:49.270 --> 00:05:53.910
I was like, "Yeah, society
needs people who are annoying,

00:05:53.910 --> 00:05:57.741
who stand outside and say,
'Yeah, but what about this?'"

00:05:57.741 --> 00:06:00.390
And I've always identified
with that kind of personality.

00:06:00.390 --> 00:06:02.700
And I do think that is somewhat

00:06:02.700 --> 00:06:05.150
of a kind of libertarian personality type.

00:06:05.150 --> 00:06:08.100
But as far as my etiology
goes in the films,

00:06:08.100 --> 00:06:10.700
I think to the extent that
there is etiology there,

00:06:10.700 --> 00:06:12.900
it's very much about humility.

00:06:12.900 --> 00:06:15.200
I think I'm a genius and
I should run the world,

00:06:15.200 --> 00:06:16.810
but I would never wanna do that

00:06:16.810 --> 00:06:19.050
because I actually don't
think that my ideas

00:06:19.050 --> 00:06:20.460
are better than other people's ideas.

00:06:20.460 --> 00:06:22.740
I mean, I think I have ideas
about how I want to live,

00:06:22.740 --> 00:06:25.490
but I'm not interested in
imposing them on other people.

00:06:25.490 --> 00:06:29.580
I think I would more say
that I'm asking people

00:06:29.580 --> 00:06:33.970
usually to consider issues
from different points of view

00:06:33.970 --> 00:06:37.110
and to acknowledge that probably
they're going to gravitate

00:06:37.110 --> 00:06:37.943
toward one of those.

00:06:37.943 --> 00:06:40.310
So you might watch the
Kenny G film and think

00:06:40.310 --> 00:06:41.770
the critics are villains.

00:06:41.770 --> 00:06:43.910
You might watch the Kenny G and think-

00:06:43.910 --> 00:06:46.590
Kenny G is history's greatest monster.

00:06:46.590 --> 00:06:50.240
Yes, someone else would
watch it and find him

00:06:50.240 --> 00:06:52.440
odious and unacceptable.

00:06:52.440 --> 00:06:54.140
I am thrilled to make a film

00:06:54.140 --> 00:06:56.130
that allows for those readings.

00:06:56.130 --> 00:07:00.170
I really am not trying to tell
people what to think, truly,

00:07:00.170 --> 00:07:01.600
but I do think that there are things

00:07:01.600 --> 00:07:03.900
that I'm passionate about foregrounding

00:07:03.900 --> 00:07:06.000
that I wanna force people to contend with.

